## I. INTRODUCTION 

The orthoferrite [RFeO3; R = rare earth] oxides have been recently re-investigated experimentally and theoretically from the fascinating perspective of multiferroicity.[1–5] Pursuing the recent line of multiferroics research, theoretical work on RFeO3 thinfilms has identified that strain can convert paraelectric phase of orthoferrites in to ferroelectrics thus rendering them multiferroic[5] . It has been found theoretically that for large values of strain on RFeO3 with large rare earth ion, giant polarization is realized. In fact, with increasing strain, a new ferroelectric phase, not observed in any perovskite before, is realized. Multifunctional properties like large magnetoelectric coupling and ultrafast optical control of spins have been observed in the orthoferrites[6–8] . The RFeO3 realize high N´eel temperature, TN ≈ 623 -740 K[9,10] however, in bulk form they are paraelectric rather than ferroelectric suggesting weak multiferroic effects. Weak ferroelectric polarization has been recently reported in Gd and Sm orthoferrites[6,11] which are thought to have ”improper” origin induced by magnetic order. In TbFeO3, an unusual incommensurate magnetic phase was discovered through – neutron diffraction[12] it was shown that the exchange of spin waves between extended topological defects could result in novel magnetic phases drawing parallels with the Yukawa forces that mediate between protons and neutrons in a nucleus. The Fe[3+] moments in TbFeO3 exhibit GxAyFz (Pb[′] n[′] m) spin configuration at room temperature[13–15] which is accompanied by a spin-reorientation to FxCyGz (Pbn[′] m[′] ). At 3 K, another spin re-orientation occurs to revert to the GxAyFz (Pb[′] n[′] m) structure. It is considered that the Tb[3+] spins order in FxCy structure in 10 - 3 K interval and in the AxGy structure below 3 K. Doping the R-site in RFeO3 with another rare earth is found to be profitable to realize electric field induced generation and reversal of ferromagnetic moments[16,17] . Chemical substitution at the Fe-site in RFeO3 also brings about interesting multiferroic effects. For example, in the case of Mn-substituted YFeO3, magnetoelectric and magnetodielectric effects at different temperatures were reported[1] . First-order spin-reorientation effects were observed as a result of Mn-substitution however, the magnetodielectric effects were observed at lower temperatures than TN or TSR. Giant magnetodielectric coupling is also observed in another doped-orthoferrite, DyMn0.33Fe0.67O3[18] . Spin-reorientation effects and magnetic sublattice effects were also observed in doped-orthoferrites with large R[19,20] . G-type magnetic ordering of Mn[3+] and Cr[3+] spins were observed below TN ≈ 84 K in the case of TbMn0.5Cr0.5O3[21] , in 

