We performed differential magnetic susceptibility measurements at 300 K, 305 K, and 310 K. This was investigated by taking numerical derivatives of the M vs H curves and, after smoothing the data, show abrupt dips in the susceptibility (with the strongest dip occurring at 310 K shown by the yellow curve in the rectangular dotted box in Fig. 6), suggestive of a phase transition consistent with [27]. AC susceptibility measurements were performed which depend upon _dMdH_[but][do][not][involve] using a numerical derivative, which can be susceptible to large fluctuations. AC measurements are therefore a much more sensitive technique, yielding a much smaller uncertainty than the above differential magnetic susceptibility measurements. The AC susceptibility measurements show similar peaks as a function of applied field, indicating a phase transition. The most pronounced dip structure is again observed at a temperature of 310 K (yellow curve in Fig. 7), consistent with Fig. 6. These dip structures are well-known markers of the temperatures and fields over which the skyrmion phase exists [27]. The AC susceptibility shows much cleaner and more defined dip structures than 6. AC measurements also probe the dynamics of the system and may be included in future work to probe the time scales of the metastable skyrmion phases found below 300 K. 

