## **3.6 The statistical distribution of lattice parameter variation** 

In polycrystalline materials such as nickel-base superalloys, the elastic and plastic deformation are highly anisotropic, giving rise to strain spatial inhomogeneity that emerges within and between grains. The inhomogeneous nature of strain distribution has led to the concept of three types of strains that describe the spatial variation over different length scales: macroscale, mesoscale and microscale. On the macroscale, the whole sample can be regarded as a homogeneous medium with averaged material properties such as Young’s modulus and yield stress. On the atomic scale (microscale), 

26 

deformation is characterized by dislocation motion, dislocation-dislocation and dislocationprecipitate interactions. In the current study, we are interested in the deformation behaviour at the mesoscale where the controlling microstructural features are grain size, morphology and orientation, 

dislocation density, phase properties, and their spatial distribution. The investigation of the distribution of lattice-parameter change at the mesoscale can effectively provide further insights into the effect of mesoscopic microstructure on mechanical properties. The lattice-parameter change 𝜀 can be defined as follows: 

