**Technical Note S1** : Development of Porod exponent equations to determine the ratio between surface areas under the two- and three- phase assumptions 

The three-phase system consists of two interfaces, one between lithium and the SEI, of surface _SL/S_ and contrast ( _ρL - ρS_ )[2] , and one between the SEI and the electrolyte, of surface _SS/E_ and contrast ( _ρS - ρE_ )[2] . The two-phase system consists of the interface between lithium metal and electrolyte of surface _SL/E_ and contrast ( _ρL - ρE_ )[2] . Expressions for the Porod exponent become: 

𝑃= 2𝜋𝑉⁄ × (𝜌𝐿 −𝜌𝐸)[2] 𝑆𝐿/𝐸 in the two-phase system 𝑃= 2𝜋𝑉⁄ × [(𝜌𝐿 −𝜌𝑆)[2] 𝑆𝐿/𝑆 + (𝜌𝑆 −𝜌𝐸)[2] 𝑆𝑆/𝐸] in the three-phase system 

Where a uniformly thick SEI coats the lithium electrode, all surfaces are equivalent with _SL/E = SL/S = SS/E = S_ . The surface area per unit volume S/V is denoted SV(2) and SV(3) in the two- and three-phase system, respectively, and equations are rearranged: 

𝑃 𝑆𝑉(2) = in the two-phase system 2𝜋×(𝜌𝐿−𝜌𝐸)[2] 

𝑃 𝑆𝑉(3) = in the three-phase system 2𝜋×[(𝜌𝐿−𝜌𝑆)[2] +(𝜌𝑆−𝜌𝐸)[2] ] 

Phase SLD are introduced into equations, with ρL = -0.82 × 10[-6] Å[-][2] , ρE = 1.73 × 10[-6] Å[-][2 ] and ρS ≈ 0.8 × 10[-6] Å[-][2] and after simplification: 

𝑆𝑉(2) = 𝑃[2𝜋× 6.5025 × 10⁄[−12] ] in the two-phase system 𝑆𝑉(3) = 𝑃[2𝜋× 3.4893 × 10⁄[−12] ] in the three-phase system 

Dividing the two, a ratio between surface areas under two- and three- phase assumptions is obtained: 

𝑆𝑉(3) =[6.5025] ≈1.86 𝑆𝑉(2) 3.4893 

