## 3.3 Powder neutron diffraction experiments 

Figures 4(a) and 4(b) show the experimental powder ND patterns for M = Al and Ga, respectively. Here, the splitting is not observed for any nuclear Bragg peaks at 2 K due to the rather low Q resolution of the instrument. Figures. 4(c) and 4(d) compare the observed intensities Iobs of nuclear Bragg peaks after the Lorentz factor correction with the calculated ones Ical based on the 4-K crystal structure revealed by the XRD (Tables II and III). A least-squares fit confirms good agreement between Iobs and Ical for both the compounds; the intensity reliability factor RB defined as RB ≡ ([�] K[|][I] obs[(][K][)][ −][I] cal[(][K][)][|][)][/][ �] K[I] obs[(][K][), where the sums are] taken over 8 nuclear reflections, is 2.78% and 4.14% for M = Al and Ga, respectively. The obtained scale factors are used for the subsequent magnetic-structure analysis. 

As shown in Figs. 4(a) and 4(b), we observe a number of additional Bragg reflections of magnetic origin at 2 K. All of theses peaks can be indexed by a single magnetic propagation vector, Q ≈ (0.89, 0.11, 0)c for CuAlCr4S8 and Q ≈ (0.81, 0.19, 0)c for CuGaCr4S8 in the cubic setting. The latter one is almost consistent with previously reported Q = (0.80, 0.18, 0)c for CuGaCr4S8.[6)] Remarkably, the identified Q vectors can be represented as Q = (qIC, 0.5, 0)o in the orthorhombic setting, where qIC ≈ 0.39 for CuAlCr4S8 and qIC ≈ 0.31 for CuGaCr4S8. Considering the exchangestriction mechanism where the AFM configuration favors a shorter Cr–Cr bond length due to the predominant direct exchange coupling, we presume that the longer-period incommensurate modulation (qa[′] = qIC) propagates along the longer diagonal (a[′] axis) and the shorter-period commensurate modulation (qb[′] = 0.5) propagates along the shorter diagonal (b[′] axis), as illustrated in Fig. 4(e). 

