Our zero-field (ZF) _µ_ SR measurements of singlecrystal MnTe reveal three distinct temperature regions below _T_ N, each showing a different magnetic response. In a ZF _µ_ SR experiment, 100% spin-polarized muons are implanted into the material, stopping at interstitial sites where they precess in the local internal magnetic field arising from the sample’s magnetism. After an average of 2.2 _µ_ s, the muon decays, emitting a positron preferentially in the direction the muon spin was pointing at the time of decay. Thus, by detecting positrons from many muon implantations, we can track the time evolution of the muon spin and understand the local magnetic field in the sample. 

