To link these experimental observations, we performed muon stopping site calculations, identifying that the muon stops at the 6g _∼_ (1 _/_ 2 _,_ 0 _,_ 0) Wyckoff site. We calculated the dipole field at this site assuming the magnetic structure of Refs. [13–15], i.e., two opposite spin sublattices. As previous work has struggled to unambiguously determine the magnetic easy axis, and the transition at _T[∗]_ likely involves the _ab_ -plane, we evaluated the dipole field for various in-plane orientations of the magnetic moments. The results are shown in Fig. 4. 

As the magnetic moment rotates in the _ab_ -plane, the number of distinct internal fields at the muon site changes. Since we detect only two fields experimentally, the moments must point along one of two directions (and their symmetric equivalents): the _a_ -axis, or 30 _[◦]_ from the _a_ -axis, which we denote as the _a[∗]_ -axis. All other directions would yield three measured internal fields. For moments along the _a_ -axis, the calculated ratio between the two internal fields is just under 2, matching our observations above _T[∗]_ . Conversely, for moments along the _a[∗]_ - axis, the ratio is closer to 5, similar to what is observed at low _T_ . This indicates that below _T[∗]_ , the magnetic moments point 30 _[◦]_ from the _a_ -axis, along _a[∗]_ (matching the previously proposed magnetic structure [15]), before undergoing a spin reorientation transition at _T[∗]_ to align with the _a_ -axis. 

Interestingly, the ratio between the two fields is still not constant below _T[∗]_ , necessitating a further, likely continuous, change to the magnetic structure. A probable explanation is a temperature-dependent rotation or canting of the magnetic moments that yields a weak net magnetization, as suggested in Ref. [8]. We find that several other subtle modifications to the magnetic structure can 

