## **3 Results and discussion** 

## **3.1 Ground state and excited state NEXAFS spectra** 

The blue line in Figure 2 shows the experimental ground state NEXAFS spectrum of AP. _≈_ The energy resolution of the spectrum ( 0.1 eV) is significantly beyond the bandwidth of the FEL pulses ( _≈_ 2.6 eV) and is obtained using spectral domain ghost imaging (see the section 2 and additional details in the SI section 1) [50]. The main peak of the spectrum at 531.0 eV corresponds to the _π[∗]_ resonance of AP, a resonant transition of an oxygen 1s electron into the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO), which has carbonyl _π[∗]_ character (see Figure 1b)). The overall shape of the measured spectrum closely resembles the spectrum of the structurally similar benzaldehyde molecule reported in the literature [55]. See the SI section 2 for a complete characterization of the spectrum. 

The orange line in Figure 2 shows the NEXAFS spectrum taken 0.5 ps after photoexcitation at 266 nm. In comparison to the static spectrum, the excitation leads to the appearance of an additional red-shifted peak at 527.0 eV and to a depletion of the ground state absorption peak at 531.0 eV, illustrated by the red and blue areas, respectively. The observed UV-induced features can be explained by a change in electron configuration in the excited sample. The UV laser excites a valence electron into a previously unoccupied molecular orbital. The single electron transition results in an electron hole in a formerly occupied molecular orbital, which in turn enables a new, red-shifted resonant transition of an oxygen 1s electron into the created hole leading to the peak at 527.0 eV. Notably, the energy difference of the UV-induced peak with respect to the _π[∗]_ resonance of _≈_ 4 eV aligns closely with the UV pump photon energy of 4.65 eV. The UV excitation also reduces the intensity of the ground state _π[∗]_ resonance, which indicates that the LUMO is half-filled in the excited state. 

