## **7 Simulated**[1] _ππ[∗]_ **state signature** 

The AIMS method used to simulate the excited-state dynamics enables analysis of the transient absorption spectra associated with each electronic state independently. The dominant feature in both the experimental and simulated data is the signature of the _nπ[∗]_ state, which exhibits a delayed onset. The initially populated _ππ[∗]_ state is challenging to observe experimentally due to its weak resonant excitation cross-section. Figure 7 a) shows the simulated transient NEXAFS spectrum of acetophenone, considering only the initially excited _ππ[∗]_ state. The intensity is plotted as a relative percentage with respect to the maximum signal of the more pronounced _nπ[∗]_ state. The positions of the _nπ[∗]_ and ground-state depletion features (taken from Figure 3 c) in the main text) are indicated by blue and red outlines, respectively. The _ππ[∗]_ state exhibits two primary contributions: the dominant feature is centered at 529.3 eV with a maximum relative intensity of approximately 13%, overlapping with the ground-state depletion at 531 eV. This overlap results in a narrowing of the depletion feature during the first 300 fs. A weaker contribution, with a relative intensity of approximately 3%, is centered at 525.6 eV, lying below the _nπ[∗]_ signature at 526.7 eV. Electronic structure calculations predict the _nπ[∗]_ state to be the lowest in energy, corresponding to an electron displacement from the HOMO to the LUMO. In contrast, the _ππ[∗]_ state involves a vacancy in a deeper-lying occupied orbital. As a result, resonant excitation from the oxygen 1s orbital to the _ππ[∗]_ state is expected 

10 

to occur at lower photon energy than to the _nπ[∗]_ state. Accordingly, the peak at 525.6 eV is assigned to direct excitation from the oxygen 1s level into a _π_ orbital, whereas the peak at 529.3 eV corresponds to excitation into the _π[∗]_ orbital of the excited state. Both features begin to decay with the onset of the _nπ[∗]_ state and fully vanish after 300 fs, leaving only minor artifacts (vertical stripes) in the data. 

