## **A. Electronic structure** 

As an initial probe for the role of SOC in the ground state wave function of Ru[3+] , XAS measurements were performed across the Ru- _L_ 2 _,_ 3-edges. Here the Ru- _L_ 2 edge corresponds to exciting 2 _p_ 1 _/_ 2 _→_ 4 _d_ while the _L_ 3 edge is 2 _p_ 3 _/_ 2 _→_ 4 _d_ . The angular part of the spin-orbit interaction _L·S_ is related to the relative intensities of the _L_ 3- and _L_ 2-edge absorption peaks [38–41] and is a conventional metric for classifying the strength of SOC in spin-orbit entangled wavefunctions [42, 43]. To investigate how the SOC in NaRuO2 compares to other strongly spin-orbit entangled systems, XAS data for NaRuO2 is plotted in Figure 2 and compared to Ru metal, as well as _α_ -RuCl3 [11]. 

XAS data at the _L_ 3 and _L_ 2 edges were modeled as discussed in the methods section to determine their branching ratio (BR) (i.e. _IL_ 3 _/IL_ 2). Fits are overplotted with the XAS spectra in Figure 2a, and the extracted branching ratios for the two measured materials are plotted in Figure 2b, alongside the BR for _α_ -RuCl3 [11]. The BR for Ru metal is around 2, which is the expected statistical BR for a metal with a quenched spin-orbit interaction. In contrast, the BR value for NaRuO2 is close to 3, similar to that found for the _J_ eff = 1 _/_ 2 state in _α_ -RuCl3 [11]. We also note similar BRs to 4 _d_ oxides with similar electron counts, namely _d_[4] SrRuO3/SrTiO3 films [44], and _d_[5] Li2RhO3 [45]. This is consistent with the picture of a _J_ eff = 1 _/_ 2 Mott state as the parent electronic phase for NaRuO2. 

Quantum chemical computations yield a BR of 2.65 for NaRuO2, somewhat lower than extracted from experiment, but still in the region corresponding to _J_ eff _≈_ 1 _/_ 2 moments. Slight underestimation of the BR was also found in quantum chemical calculations on e. g. Ba2IrO4 [34]. For comparison, a BR of 2.9 is obtained computationally for RuCl3; experimentally, values of 3 [11] and 3.6 [46] were reported for the latter compound. The smaller BR for NaRuO2 in the embedded-cluster quantum chemical computations can be understood on the basis of two effects : (i) a larger trigonal splitting ( _≈_ 0.11 eV in NaRuO2 [27] vs _≈_ 0.07 eV in RuCl3 [33]), which yields a BR lower than the 2.75 value corresponding to pristine, cubic-environment _J_ eff =1 _/_ 2 moments with negligible configuration-interaction effects and 2nd-order SOCs within the 4 _d_[5] manifold; (ii) a larger octahedral _t_ 2 _g_ - _eg_ splitting, with _t_ 2 _g_ -to- _eg_ excitations starting at _≈_ 1.6 eV in NaRuO2 (see [27] and next paragraphs) and at _≈_ 1.3 eV in RuCl3 [33, 46], which makes configuration-interaction effects and 2nd-order SOCs somewhat less effective in NaRuO2. 

