recovering the magnetization of a thin film from strayfield magnetometry [21]. We extend X-ray magnetic vector tomography with the complete micromagnetic energy functional of the sample and demonstrate its two roles on a thick disk of the room-temperature chiral magnet Co8Zn9Mn3 [22], imaged with dichroic soft X-ray ptychography. First, the energy encodes our prior knowledge of the interactions in the material and helps us recover the magnetization vector field where a limited number of noisy projections alone is not enough. Second, because the reconstruction explicitly incorporates these competing interactions, it allows us to test the assumed model itself. Since material parameters are usually only determined for the bulk and do not always translate to a nanostructure, we identify the relevant parameters of the sample that best reproduce the measured projections. This prospect of texture-driven magnetometry on individual nanostructures is relevant for a wide variety of magnetic material systems. 

In magnetic tomography, XMCD imaging records two projections at every sample orientation, with opposite circular X-ray polarization _σ[±]_ , giving a transmission image pair _C_[+] and _C[−]_ [8, 23]. At the X-ray absorption edges of magnetic materials, the XMCD effect provides contrast that is sensitive to the local magnetization component along the beam propagation direction [7] and reverses sign between _σ_[+] and _σ[−]_ . We sum _C_[+] and _C[−]_ to yield a purely electronic channel _c_ and subtract them to yield a magnetic (XMCD) channel _y_ (Fig. 1b and Methods). Because XMCD contrast is proportional to the magnetization component parallel to the beam propagation direction, each measured projection **y** _i_ records the line integral of that component along the beam [8]. In our geometry, the beam stays along **z** ˆ while the stage rotates the sample by **R** _i_ (Fig. 1a), so in the sample frame the beam direction is **k**[ˆ] _i_ = **R** _[T] i_ **[z]**[ˆ][[9].][The][forward][model] 

