Unlike the scalar density imaged in conventional tomography, the magnetization is a vector field probed only along the beam, so rotating the sample changes not only the viewing angle but also which component is sensed. To reconstruct all three vector components, the acquisition therefore needs to combine two orthogonal tilt series (Fig. 1a) or to use a laminography geometry [11]. Tilting about _y_ ˆ confines the beam to the _xz_ plane, so this series (the _xz_ series) is sensitive to _mx_ and _mz_ but never to ˆ _my_ . Tilting about _x_ confines the beam to the _yz_ plane, so the _yz_ series is sensitive to _my_ and _mz_ but never to _mx_ . 

Recovering the three-dimensional magnetization configuration requires finding a vector field whose forward projections reproduce the measured data (Fig. 1c). Even a complete dual-axis acquisition does not uniquely determine the magnetization: line integrals of **m** _·_ **k**[ˆ] determine at most the divergence-free part of the magnetization [25, 26], since any internal gradient field that vanishes at the sample boundary integrates to zero along every beam path [9]. In physical terms, the magnetic volume and surface charges of the sample cannot be fully recovered without additional constraints. In practice, depending on the sample geometry and the setup, projections cannot always be recorded over the complete angular range, leaving a so-called missing wedge of unrecorded viewing directions in tomography and a missing cone in laminography [27]. Moreover, weak XMCD signals make the projections particularly susceptible to noise and imaging artifacts across the angular range. 

Despite these challenges, iterative magnetic vector tomography [8, 9] has recovered three-dimensional magnetic textures by minimizing the data misfit between simulated and measured projections. However, without further constraints, these limitations can leave nanoscale features poorly resolved and introduce artifacts into the reconstruction. We therefore add the micromagnetic energy of the material to this data misfit and minimize the combined objective function _L_ ( **m** ), 

