Our readout lies 10 % above the literature value _D_ 0, although our disk winds with the same 120 nm period (Fig. 4d) as the nanowire from which _D_ 0 was derived [20]. The literature value follows from inserting this period into the bulk relation _D_ = 4 _πA_ 0 _/L_ , which describes a helix in an unbounded crystal, whereas our readout models the full micromagnetic energy of the confined disk. We test this difference with the synthetic disk, whose _D_ is known: relaxed at _A_ = _A_ 0 and _D_ = 0 _._ 66 _×_ 10 _[−]_[3] J m _[−]_[2] (Methods), it forms stripes with a 122 nm period, longer than the 109 nm that the bulk relation predicts. Applied to this period, the bulk relation underestimates _D_ by about 10 %, as in the measured disk. Our readout, in contrast, returns the _D_ used to relax the synthetic disk from its simulated projections (Extended Data Fig. 1f). 

Even at the optimal _D_ , the high-weight reconstruction ( _λ[E]_ high[)][has][a][larger][data][misfit][than][the][balanced][recon-] struction ( _L_ data = 0 _._ 93 versus 0 _._ 83, Fig. 2a). In a real sample, however, pinning sites, local defects, or focusedion-beam damage can distort the texture in ways that our energy model does not capture, increasing the data misfit at _λ[E]_ high[.] In our disk these local distortions are small enough that the overall texture still lies close to a minimum of Eq. (4), allowing a clear dip in _L_ data. If strong pinning instead traps a sample in a metastable state that Eq. (4) cannot reproduce, the high-weight reconstruction converges to a state far from the measured texture for every parameter set, leaving _L_ data uniformly high and preventing a readout. 

which the data misfit can be used to read out the parameters that shape the texture. Moreover, a parameter’s impact on the reconstruction matches its sensitivity in the readout: parameters that barely affect the texture cannot be extracted, but they also barely change the reconstructed state, whereas parameters that strongly shape the texture can be determined directly from the misfit. In the helimagnetic Co8Zn9Mn3 disk, the reconstruction is relatively insensitive to _M_ s, whereas the ratio _A/D_ sets the period of the helical winding. At the literature exchange stiffness _A_ 0, the readout returns _D_ = 0 _._ 66 _×_ 10 _[−]_[3] J m _[−]_[2] , 10 % above _D_ 0, although the disk winds with the 120 nm period from which _D_ 0 was derived. The bulk relation behind _D_ 0 describes an unbounded crystal and underestimates _D_ in the confined disk. The ratio _A/D_ therefore has to be measured in the nanostructure itself. 

The sparse-data recovery and the parameter readout together suggest a strategy for experimental design. Dense dual-axis tilt series, typically required for highresolution three-dimensional imaging, take a significant amount of beamtime in vector tomography. With the physics prior, far fewer projections are required, and even a single tilt axis can provide a robust reconstruction. Every projection a reconstruction can spare buys beamtime for additional specimens, magnetic fields, or time points within one allocation, and thus for more complex experiments such as in situ studies. A single well-sampled measurement of a prepared reference state, such as a helical texture whose period pins the constants, could then validate the energy model and fix the material parameters, while subsequent conditions are imaged with sparse acquisitions. 

We demonstrate this physics-informed approach with XMCD tomography, but automatic differentiation in micromagnetic software [28, 35] allows the physics prior to be paired with any differentiable forward model and extended with further interactions of the host material. The same approach can therefore be extended to other magnetic imaging modalities, including electron holographic and Lorentz tomography [12, 13] and laminography [11]. 

