## **V. CONCLUSIONS** 

We have presented an approach to imaging nanoscale magnetic textures that incorporates the micromagnetic energy into the objective function, with a single weight _λ[E]_ selecting between two operating regimes of the same reconstruction. In the balanced regime the prior helps recover the texture from noisy, incomplete measurements, and the result tolerates the typical uncertainty of the assumed material parameters. A larger weight on the micromagnetic energy switches to the second regime, in 

_a. Sample._ The Co8Zn9Mn3 disk of Extended Data Table I sits on a focused-ion-beam support wedge. The disk was cut from a single crystal grown at the University of Warwick by the Bridgman method [14]. The table gives its acquisition and discretization. The material parameters _M_ s, _A_ 0, and _D_ 0 are those of Ref. [20] for the same compound: _M_ s from dc magnetization data, and _A_ 0 with _D_ 0 chosen there to reproduce the helical period _LD_ = 4 _πA_ 0 _/D_ 0 = 120 nm imaged in a 200 nm thick nanowire. 

8 

_b. X-ray data acquisition._ We acquired the projections at room temperature using dichroic soft X-ray ptychography [8, 23] on the I08-1 ptychography end station of Diamond Light Source under proposal MG37535-2. An APPLE-II undulator produced circularly polarized X-rays of either helicity, set by its gap and phase, and the Au 600 lines _/_ mm grating of the SX-700 plane-grating monochromator [36] tuned them 3 eV below the Co _L_ 3 edge (778 _._ 1 eV). A 10 µm pinhole defined the secondary source, and a Fresnel zone plate with a diameter of 333 µm, an outermost zone width of 70 nm, and a mean focal length of 14 _._ 156 mm between 770 and 807 eV focused the beam. The sample sat on an L-bracket on an SR-5714C rotation stage (SmarAct GmbH), about 50 µm downstream of the focus, and we scanned it through the beam along a spiral path to avoid grid artifacts. An in-vacuum Axis-SXRF-2020EUV sCMOS detector (Axis Photonique Inc.) recorded the diffraction patterns. We measured the two polarizations separately as _C_[+] and _C[−]_ , each yielding a series of diffraction patterns. At the absorption peak, resonant attenuation would block transmission through the disk, whereas at this pre-edge energy the transmission remains high while the magnetic phase contrast persists [14]. We reconstructed the complexvalued object projections (amplitude and phase contrast images) and the probe with PtyPy [37] after cropping the diffraction patterns to 1024 _×_ 1024 pixels and binning them to 512 _×_ 512 pixels, running 1000 iterations of the relaxed averaged alternating reflections (RAAR) engine, 200 iterations of maximum-likelihood refinement, and another 500 RAAR iterations. 

_c. Preprocessing._ Each complex projection carries an absorptive channel (the log amplitude) and a dispersive channel (the phase). In either channel, we combine the projections of the two polarizations to isolate the electronic term _c_ and the XMCD term _y_ , 

**==> picture [218 x 13] intentionally omitted <==**

Both encode the same projected moment, but at the preedge energy the phase XMCD has the higher signal-tonoise ratio, and therefore is selected as the reconstruction input. Starting from a coarse pre-alignment of the projections, we fit an ellipse to the projected disk outline in every frame and shift its center onto the rotation axis [38], register the exposures of each angle (both polarizations and any repeats) against each other on the electronic channel before averaging them, and apply the composed shift in a single cubic interpolation per exposure. We then remove the phase ramp of every projection by fitting a plane to the phase over the air region, detected by an Otsu threshold [39] on the transmission amplitude, and subtracting it. For the electronic channel, we subtract from _c_ its median outside the disk support in every frame, which normalizes the transmitted amplitude to the background. 

_d. Tilt geometry._ The stage angles enter the forward model through one rotation per series about its axis. We calibrate the zero point _θ_ 0 of each series 

from the same disk outlines. The disk outline is a circle when the disk faces the beam and an ellipse that narrows as the disk tilts away. We fit the aspect ratio of this ellipse in all frames of a series at once with _a_ ( _θ_ ) = _s_ [cos( _θ_ + _θ_ 0) + _k |_ sin( _θ_ + _θ_ 0) _|_ ], where _θ_ is the stage angle recorded with each frame in the raw data. The fit has three free parameters: the zero point _θ_ 0, a scale _s_ , and _k_ , which accounts for the blurred, partly absorbing rim. We apply _θ_ 0 rounded to the nearest degree, the precision of the stage labels (Extended Data Table I). 

