Fig. 1a). We relax this state with the Landau–Lifshitz– Gilbert equation [32, 33] in NeuralMag [28] to equilibrium at the Extended Data Table I parameters with _D_ = 0 _._ 66 _×_ 10 _[−]_[3] J m _[−]_[2] , and block-average it onto the tomography grid. The relaxation turns the noisy seed into a smooth labyrinth of stripes that follows the arrangement of the measured texture (Extended Data Fig. 1a). 

_n. Measurement simulation._ We project this state with the forward model of Eq. (7), the operator the reconstruction inverts, at the 29 + 38 calibrated stage angles of the measurement and at the magnetization scale _γ_ of the reconstructions, so the measured noise amplitude carries over directly. The noise is a stationary Gaussian field with the radially averaged power spectrum of aironly patches of the measured XMCD channel, scaled to the median standard deviation of the measured air pixels, and added with opposite sign to the two polarizations. 

_o. Evaluation._ We reconstruct the synthetic projections using the same solver settings and disk mask as the experimental data. Fidelity is quantified by the relative reconstruction error _εm_ = _∥_ **m** _[⋆] −_ **m**[GT] _∥/∥_ **m**[GT] _∥_ , evaluated inside the disk mask. 

